{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a persistent rise (new 20–30d highs), the model systematically underestimates the level over the full horizon. Apply an additive uplift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–3, larger by days 10–30).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors increase with lead time (roughly monotone). Use a linear ramp correction: +k * horizon_day, where k is proportional to recent slope (e.g., 10-day return/10).",
    "Trend lag: forecasts behave like a smoothed/lagged version of reality (turning points occur later). If momentum is positive (e.g., 5d MA > 20d MA and accelerating), shift the forecast path upward and slightly earlier (front-load part of the uplift into days 1–7).",
    "Post-breakout persistence mis-modeled: after a breakout above prior range, the model mean-reverts too quickly and caps prices ~70–76 while outcomes persist ~78–84. Add a regime switch: once price closes above prior 20d high, reduce mean-reversion strength and keep forecasts closer to a higher plateau.",
    "Late-horizon peak underestimation: the model misses late-horizon highs during rallies. Apply a convex uplift (increasing more than linearly) for days 15–30 when recent volatility + momentum are both elevated."
  ]
}
